(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608150200")
  (:adr/title "shinshi-video-actor: residency belongs to the machine that holds the GPU, not to the Bots surface")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-15")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

The owner asked to make shinshi.club's catalog video generation resident —
phrased as *「itonami.cloud の bots の常駐化」* — and approved placing the
publish write-key on gad.

Before this, the generation existed as a Claude skill
(`.claude/skills/shinshi-catalog-video`) plus three scripts in the superproject,
driven by hand. **The launchd job the skill describes as ticking every 30
minutes was never installed**: `com.gftd.shinshi-catalog-video` is absent from
`launchctl list`, and `~/.gftd/shinshi-catalog-video/loop.ledger.edn` contained
only entries written by hand on 2026-08-15. So there was no residency to move —
there was a documented residency that did not exist.

## Decision

Create `cloud-itonami/shinshi-video-actor`, the second service cloud-itonami
operates for club-shinshi (sibling of `shinshi-growth-actor`), and run it as a
**systemd timer on gad** — the machine that already holds the ComfyUI this
generation drives.

Registered in west as `cloud-itonami-shinshi-video-actor` at
`orgs/cloud-itonami/shinshi-video-actor`, pin `96288001`, by single-entry API
commit `e216c3bc` (4177 -> 4178 entries, no other pin touched).

### Why not a Bot in cloud-itonami-app

That surface is deliberately this laptop. `bots.clj` says so in its own words:
*'What a Bot's own computer is here — Not a cloud VM … A Bot's computer is
therefore this machine.'* That is the right answer for a Bot reading mail, and
the wrong one here: the work happens on a GPU box and on Cloudflare, and the
laptop only issues instructions. Under ADR-2608111721's three questions, the
answer to *'what stops if this machine sleeps tonight'* should be **nothing**.

The Bots surface remains the right place to *look at* and *steer* the actor.
This ADR separates where it is watched from where it lives, which is the same
split ADR-2608111721 draws: 常駐の機構は murakumo、常駐する権利は cloud-itonami.

### Why gad and not asher

asher is the only host declared in `probe.cljs`'s `operator-hosts` — the
implementation of `:slot/attested`. Measured 2026-08-15, **asher has no
outbound HTTPS and cannot reach gad's ComfyUI**: `api.cloudflare.com`,
`shinshi.club` and `100.82.98.110:8188` all returned 000. It cannot publish and
it cannot generate.

gad answered `shinshi.club:200` and `api.cloudflare.com:400` (reachable),
already carries `nbb`, `node` and `wrangler`, runs the ComfyUI locally, and has
been up 29 days under systemd. It is **not in `scripts/fleet-ci/nodes.edn`**, so
no gate code sent from a repository is ever scheduled onto it — which is the
substance of fleet invariant 3, not merely its letter.

**gad already holds a write key**: the Radicle seed COB signing identity
(`gates.edn` `:rad`, passphrase item `radicle-seed-gad-passphrase`). So this
does not create a second attested host — but it does surface that gad has been
attested in substance while only asher was attested in declaration.
`operator-hosts` should name gad. Not doing so is safe today only because gad is
absent from `nodes.edn`, which `probe.cljs`'s own docstring calls out as luck
rather than design.

## The actor

`generation ⊣ CatalogGovernor ⊣ append-only ledger`, driven by a durable outer
loop rather than a StateGraph — the same reasoning ADR-2607123000 §3 gives for
the seven sibling `gftd-*-actor` asset actors: a generation is minutes-scale and
the process may die inside one, so recovery is running the tick again.

Two structural choices worth recording:

**Measurement and judgement are separate namespaces.** `measure` runs ffprobe
and returns numbers or `:unanswered true`; `governor` is pure and consumes that.
A gate that fuses them must map 'could not measure' onto pass or fail, and in
this workspace that mapping has always eventually become pass (ADR-2608136000).
Here an unanswered measurement is simply a value the governor rejects.

**The IP containment is upstream of the governor, and it is load-bearing.**
While building `catalog/series-of`, a version that read only the slug suffix
classified `tifa-final-fantasy` as `Astral Realm` — `%-fantasy` matches
`-final-fantasy` — and put Square Enix's Tifa at the head of the candidate
queue. **The governor does not catch this**: the candidate arrives already
carrying an original series name. Caught by running the selector against live
D1 rather than by reading it. The profile's declared series now wins and a
declared non-original series is a refusal rather than a fallback; both
directions are in `test/run.cljs`, which also asserts that the governor's and
the selector's copies of the 12 series are the same set.

## Verified on gad

- 27-case test suite: ALL OK (governor accept + one reject per check, plus the
  `series-of` regression cases)
- `measure` on a real clip: same bytes/frames/geometry as the laptop
- `install.cljs` refuses correctly (exit 2) with the env file absent
- ffmpeg/ffprobe installed (`7:6.1.1-3ubuntu5`); gad had neither

**Measurements are not comparable across hosts.** The same clip reads
140.945 luma / 9.508 motion under macOS ffmpeg 7.x and 141.641 / 9.563 under
gad's 6.1.1. Thresholds are 8 and 1, so no verdict changes — but a stored
number must not be diffed against a fresh one and read as drift.

## The actor's own plane is kotobase.net (owner instruction, same day)

Asked to use kotobase.net's API rather than D1, the actor now mints a CACAO
from its **own** Ed25519 key and keeps its ledger -- every commit and every
hold -- on the kotobase.net datom plane. Verified live with a freshly
generated seed: transact wrote, the read returned the rows, `policy-mode:
legacy-public`. **No owner credential, no Cloudflare token, no hand-off** --
the third clause of ADR-2608111721 (hokan wa kotobase) made concrete.

A full tick then ran end to end and published `aoi-hoshino-seaside` --
generated, measured, passed the governor, uploaded, verified live (200
video/mp4, 234344 B, present in the character's feed), and recorded itself on
the plane. That is the fifth catalog entry filled and the first the actor
filled by itself.

### What did NOT move, and why it is not a detail

Candidate selection and the feed-post write still speak the appview's D1.
**shinshi.club's appview reads D1 and nothing else** -- its `kotobase`
references are comments, measured. Writing the post to kotobase instead would
make the clip durably recorded and permanently invisible, which is worse than
leaving it. Moving them needs the appview to gain a kotobase read path, or a
token-gated record route beside `/b/upload`; either is a change to a live
shared Worker and is not an actor change.

So the credential story is split and should be stated that way: **the actor's
own memory needs nothing**, and the appview conversation still needs a
Cloudflare token scoped to `ai-gftd-shinshi` and `ai-gftd-pds-recordlog`.

## Four bugs, all found by running it rather than reading it

Recorded because they are one shape -- a check that cannot fire returning the
value of a check that looked and found nothing (ADR-2608136000):

1. **`series-of` on the slug suffix alone** classified `tifa-final-fantasy` as
   `Astral Realm` and put Square Enix's Tifa at the head of the queue. The
   governor cannot catch it: the candidate arrives already carrying an original
   series name. Caught against live D1.
2. **`entries` queried `ledger/kind` while `ledger-summary` folds on
   `ledger/tag`.** The fold received rows it could never match and returned
   `{:published 0 :recent-failures #{}}` against a non-empty ledger -- the
   budget could never bite and a failing slug was retried immediately -- while
   the tick printed `RECORDED true`. The test now feeds WRONG-predicate rows
   and asserts zero; a test using only right-predicate rows passes against the
   broken reader too.
3. **`comfy-free?` read `MURAKUMO_COMFY_URL` while the vendored python read
   `COMFY`**, so a tick could confirm a healthy GPU on one host and submit the
   graph to another.
4. **gad runs node 18.19.1**, which has no `globalThis.crypto` (added in v19)
   and no happy-eyeballs in `fetch`. The first broke CACAO minting; the second
   made every kotobase.net call fail `ECONNREFUSED` while `curl` to the same
   URL returned 200 from the same shell. Shimmed, and stated in the unit.

Number 4 deserves its own line: **`--whoami` worked on gad throughout**,
because deriving a public key from a seed needs no randomness. Only the first
call that actually authenticated failed. A smoke test that stops at 'the
identity loads' would have reported a healthy deployment.

## The Cloudflare credential is gone, and the timer is running

The actor's last two D1 calls -- the candidate scan and the feed-post write --
were the appview's own data reached through a Cloudflare token that would have
had to sit on a GPU box. Both moved into the appview Worker, where the D1
bindings already live, as two XRPC methods
(`network-awai/club-shinshi-app` commit `77ee2134`, deployed):

- **`listUnfilled`** -- the candidate query. Scan limit 600, above the 593 rows
  that qualify, and it reports `truncated`.
- **`projectVideoPost`** -- token-gated on `UPLOAD_TOKEN`, checked BEFORE the
  d1-direct branch so a rename cannot make it fall through unauthenticated, and
  it does not accept a browser session.

The actor's three writes now use: this appview's upload token (the clip's
bytes), the actor's **own** Ed25519 key (the record, to kotobase), and that same
upload token (the projection). **No owner credential anywhere.**

`projectVideoPost` requires `kotobaseCid` and refuses without it. That is what
keeps the D1 row a projection rather than a second original: kotobase holds the
authoritative copy, the row names it, and the row is rebuildable from the
graph. Verified on the live row for `emi-hoshino-cafe` -- its `kotobaseCid` is
`bafyreiaqylryjwixp6h64o3zc7omupyapqelrubikvchrevlsk5xb74lfi`.

The write order is R2 -> kotobase -> projection, chosen so every partial failure
is repairable. A failed commit leaves an orphaned blob (invisible, cheap,
re-runnable); a failed projection leaves a durable record a later call renders.
The reverse would put a visible post in front of a record that exists nowhere
authoritative, which retrying cannot fix.

`series-of` moved with the candidate list, because a check downstream of the
list cannot see a candidate that arrives already carrying an original series
name. It has cases in both repositories now.

**The timer is enabled.** `deploy/install.cljs --apply` ran on gad and the
first unattended firing published `emi-hoshino-campus` by itself. Next tick
every 30 minutes.

## Six more bugs, all found by running it

Every one is the same shape -- a check that cannot fire returning the value of
a check that looked and found nothing (ADR-2608136000):

1. **`series-of` on the slug suffix alone** put Square Enix's Tifa at the head
   of the queue as an `Astral Realm` original.
2. **`entries` queried `ledger/kind` while `ledger-summary` folds on
   `ledger/tag`** -- the budget could never bite and a failing slug was retried
   at once, while the tick printed `RECORDED true`.
3. **`comfy-free?` read `MURAKUMO_COMFY_URL`, the vendored python read
   `COMFY`** -- a tick could confirm a healthy GPU on one host and submit to
   another.
4. **node 18 on gad** has no `globalThis.crypto` and no happy-eyeballs in
   `fetch`. `--whoami` kept working throughout, because deriving a pubkey from
   a seed needs no randomness; only the first call that AUTHENTICATED failed.
5. **`.catch` threaded inside its own `.then`** in the appview's candidate
   scan. It compiled -- the parens balanced, the meaning did not -- and failed
   only in production, as `(intermediate value).catch is not a function`.
6. **A cljs.test async test that asserted nothing.** Written without
   `cljs.test/async`, the reporter finished before the `.then` ran: the suite
   went green claiming an extra passing test while the assertion total stayed
   at 3447. Fixed, it is 3454, and breaking the chain again reproduces exactly
   the production error before restoring it.

Number 6 deserves the emphasis. It was a test written **to cover** number 5,
and in its first form it could not have caught it. The assertion count is what
exposed it -- not the test result, which was green either way.

## Closing state (2026-08-15)

Landed and verified, all on default branches:

| what | where |
|---|---|
| actor repo | `cloud-itonami/shinshi-video-actor` @ `5fc4db24` |
| west entry + pin | `com-junkawasaki/root` @ `a6bb5535`, pin `5fc4db24` |
| catalog-loop fix + run 0037 | `com-junkawasaki/root` @ `6744c6c1` |
| appview bridge | `network-awai/club-shinshi-app` @ `77ee2134` |
| appview fixes matching production | same @ `29e787b3` |
| deployed Worker | version `9dbd1261` |

Checked at close: the actor checkout, the west pin and origin/main agree on
`5fc4db24`; the appview checkout, its main and the deployed Worker source are
byte-identical; all five actor source files on gad hash-match the landed
versions; the timer is enabled with its next firing scheduled.

**One gap was found by this audit and closed.** Three fixes -- the D1
bound-parameter limit, the misthreaded `.catch`, and the async test -- were made
in the shared checkout AFTER the first merge, then built and deployed.
Production was correct and main was not. Landing them was not tidying: a later
deploy from main would have silently reintroduced
`D1_ERROR: too many SQL variables` on the live endpoint.

### Resume point

The actor fills one character per 30-minute tick, unattended. **90 remain**;
the head of the queue is `emi-hoshino-fantasy`.

- On gad: `nbb --classpath src:/home/gad/kotobase-client/src bin/resident.cljs --status`
  (needs `NODE_OPTIONS=--dns-result-order=ipv4first` outside the unit)
- From anywhere: `POST https://shinshi.club/xrpc/ai.gftd.apps.shinshi.listUnfilled`

`--status` reporting `ANSWERED false` means the plane could not be read, NOT an
empty ledger; `listUnfilled` returning `unanswered` means the same for the
catalogue. Neither is a reason to generate.

### Known gaps, in priority order

1. **`probe.cljs`'s `operator-hosts` does not name gad**, which now holds a
   publish credential as well as its Radicle signing key. It is safe today only
   because gad is absent from `nodes.edn` -- which that docstring itself calls
   luck rather than design.
2. **The projection is push-only.** Nothing rebuilds the recordlog from the
   kotobase graph, so the claim that D1 is reconstructible is true in principle
   and untested in practice. A `reprojectFromKotobase` pass would make it real.
3. **`motion` has no upper band.** 4.2 and 21.0 both pass; the first is close to
   a still. A yellow band would surface it without failing the clip.
4. **IP characters remain excluded** and are the larger share of the catalogue.
   Unblocking them is a licensing decision, not an engineering one.

## Consequences

- The catalog fill stops depending on the laptop being awake, once the
  credential lands.
- The actor's own plane needs no credential at all. One machine will still hold a publish credential for the appview half. It is the machine that already
  held a signing key and already cannot receive gate work.
- The 12 original series are now asserted identical in two places by test. IP
  characters are excluded twice, independently, and the outer exclusion is the
  one that actually matters.
- `operator-hosts` in `probe.cljs` should gain gad. Filed as follow-up, not
  done here: changing gate rotation membership mid-session without re-running
  probe is exactly the ordering hazard that docstring warns about.")))
